{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"=== HANDOFF #12 — epic e369 (Jira DC) + parity epic 3e73. READ THIS, THEN #11, THEN #10. ===\n\nWORKTREE /Users/joeoakhart/rebar-jira-family @ df41edcf25 (== origin/main at writing), clean.\nALWAYS: export PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\". DO NOT trust the board below — read the tickets.\n\n*** THE SINGLE MOST IMPORTANT OPERATIONAL FACT THIS SESSION ***\nA CONCURRENT SESSION'S PLAN-REVIEW QUEUE AGENT IS REVIEWING MY TICKETS. Observed directly via\n`ps -Ao args | grep review-plan`: it certified 79d5, then moved to 2944, and per handoff #11 its\nqueue ENDS AT cc77. DO NOT duplicate its reviews — that is doubled LLM spend AND it is the source of\nthe store contention described below. CHECK `ps` FOR RUNNING `review-plan` BEFORE STARTING ONE.\n\n--- BOARD (verified at writing, NOT remembered) ---\ne369: 26/30 closed. OPEN: 727f + d103 (P1, fix WRITTEN AND PUSHED), 5200 + cc77 (P2).\n3e73: 0/6 closed. P1: cedc, 79d5, bd9e, 2944. P2: 0fad, 2127 (Cloud-side; must NOT gate DC).\nATTESTATIONS: 79d5 CERTIFIED. cedc stale-material. bd9e stale-head. 2944 unsigned (being reviewed).\n  3e73 epic: PASSED but UNSIGNED (see the generation trap below). e369: stale-code (22 files).\n  727f/d103: unsigned, but already claimed — closes go through the completion-verifier, not\n  plan-review, so that is fine.\n\n--- 727f + d103 ARE DONE AND CORRECT. THEY ARE WAITING ONLY ON CI. ---\nGerrit change 1209, LLM-Review +1. Both tickets in_progress; DO NOT close until a real Verified +1.\nThe blocking question handoff #11 flagged was resolved as option (b): BOTH failing orchestrate tests\npinned the MECHANISM, not behaviour. I re-verified the change myself as a second reader because the\noperator's instruction was \"do not resolve it by relaxing assertions\":\n  * WHOLE-CHANGE LEDGER: 19 asserts ADDED, 1 REMOVED. Net +18.\n  * The one removal (`mutation_count == 1` in test_real_field_change_converges_after_one_pass) was\n    PINNING THE DEFECT: the fixture edits the summary JIRA-SIDE with local_tickets=[], so the single\n    mutation counted was an outbound update carrying the STALE value — a REVERT of the fixture's own\n    edit. Its intent survives as a pass-1 `>= 1` over-filtering guard, and pass 2 now asserts a\n    strict `== 0`. Tighter, not looser.\n  * The second touched cell was VACUOUS: `_make_stub_differ` returned the test's OWN list, the diff\n    phase appends in place, so `actual_count == len(mutations)` compared an aliased list to itself\n    and held for ANY number of appended mutations. Now returns a copy + gained an assertion.\nNEXT ACTION: watch 1209 for Verified +1, submit, `git fetch origin && git merge --ff-only`, then\nclose 727f and d103. CI WAS BADLY CONGESTED (30 queued runs from a 16:21 burst) but drains FIFO — it\nwent 30 -> 5 within the session. The runs are NOT starved: they complete 14/15 jobs and only the\nvote-casting job queues. DO NOT cancel other changes' runs to jump the queue.\n\n--- TWO GATE TRAPS I HIT. BOTH COST ME REVIEW ROUNDS. CARRY THEM. ---\n1. `rebar set-file-impact` IS A MATERIAL PLAN EDIT AND STALES A CERTIFIED ATTESTATION.\n   I declared file_impact on all four 3e73 children to satisfy an advisory — AFTER cedc had been\n   certified — and turned cedc from `certified` straight to `stale-material`. DECLARE FILE IMPACT\n   BEFORE THE REVIEW, NEVER AFTER. (I did NOT use `sign-review` to paper over it: cedc's recorded\n   review scored criterion P9 against UNDECLARED file impact, so re-signing would certify a P9\n   assessment nobody made — a milder version of the cc77 sidecar hazard.)\n2. REVIEW CHILDREN BEFORE THE PARENT — AGENTS.md says this and I inverted it.\n   The 3e73 epic review PASSED and then REFUSED TO SIGN with\n   `PlanReviewGenerationChanged: a dependency's plan material changed since the review`\n   (non-retryable; the tool tells you to re-review, so `sign-review` is WRONG here). An epic review\n   PINS ALL ITS CHILDREN'S MATERIAL, and a child being CERTIFIED mid-flight changes that child's\n   generation. With another agent certifying children concurrently, a parent review will keep\n   losing this race. CERTIFY ALL CHILDREN FIRST, REVIEW THE EPIC LAST.\n\n--- STORE CONTENTION IS CURRENTLY DEFEATING cedc's REVIEW ---\nThree consecutive `review-plan cedc` runs returned INDETERMINATE with\n`plan relation snapshot failed ... reason: store-read-failure`, `canonical_id: None`,\n`reference: <.tickets-tracker path>`. READ THAT CAREFULLY: the reference is the STORE PATH, not a\nticket, so it is a failure to READ THE STORE (contention), NOT an unreadable relation — do not go\nhunting for a corrupt link. llm_ran was False, so no LLM spend was wasted and there is no PASS to\nsign. It is transient: the epic review succeeded earlier under the same conditions. RETRY WHEN `ps`\nSHOWS NO OTHER review-plan RUNNING.\n`rebar doctor` reports 14 pre-existing findings (mis-escalated links, plus\n`ancestor-blocking: e369 depends_on 9263`, a descendant). I did NOT run `--repair`: it mutates links\nrepo-wide across other sessions' tickets and none of the findings is implicated in this failure.\n\n--- WORK COMPLETED THIS SESSION THAT IS NOT A TICKET CLOSURE ---\nAll of the following is recorded ON the tickets (each verified landed by byte length — remember\n`rebar comment` is POSITIONAL and a stray `--body` records the literal string and exits 0):\n * 3e73's description gained a proper `## Acceptance criteria` block (8 items) — the epic was\n   BLOCKING on the deterministic readiness floor (P1 readiness-shape) for having none. The\n   mutation-check AC is tagged `[operator-attested]` because a mutation is applied and reverted in a\n   working tree and leaves no repo artifact for CI to inspect.\n * file_impact declared on cedc / 79d5 / bd9e / 2944 (see trap 1 for the cost).\n * cc77's REWRITTEN PREMISE INDEPENDENTLY CONFIRMED. Probe placed INSIDE the package so only the\n   import path varied: shim import -> `Any`, bogus call NO ERROR; relative import -> real\n   TicketTransport, bogus call `[attr-defined]`, AND `set_entity_property` accepted (no false\n   positive). THE METACLASS IS EXONERATED against today's class. cc77 is safe to implement as\n   written. STILL TRUE: NEVER `sign-review` cc77 — its sidecar predates the rewrite.\n * cedc's two hardest ACs verified sound: `FakeTransport.probe_remote` really does return\n   `ProbeBranch.UNREACHABLE` UNCONDITIONALLY (so `_backend()`-seam probe pins are unfalsifiable),\n   and DC's `DEFAULT_RESOLVED_STATUSES` is BYTE-IDENTICAL to Cloud's `RESOLVED_STATUS_NAMES`\n   (both `frozenset({\"Resolved\",\"Done\",\"Cancelled\"})`) — so AC6's demand for a NON-DEFAULT set is\n   load-bearing, not belt-and-braces. An implementer who \"simplifies\" to defaults reintroduces the\n   vacuity this epic exists to remove.\n * bd9e de-risked: the two `_LOCAL_TO_JIRA_TYPE` copies are CONTENT-IDENTICAL (differ only in key\n   order), so unification is BEHAVIOUR-PRESERVING and needs no divergence decision. All 7 grep sites\n   match the plan; `config.py:11` is a comment, and the module-attribute consumer at\n   test_outbound_differ_session_log_exclusion.py:87 is why the historical-alias import is mandatory.\n * 2944 item 3 triaged: NOT a product bug. DC's `validate_assignee_exists` is `-> str` with exactly\n   two exits — `return str(_user_attr(user,\"name\") or assignee)` or `raise AssigneeNotFoundError`.\n   Pure test gap; no bug to file. Check Cloud (acli.py:541) separately — its contract is\n   deliberately different (accountId).\n * 2944 item 2 confirmed AND a constraint the plan omits: the teeth test cannot simply \"call the\n   production helper\", because `_search_issues_calls_taking_the_default()` takes NO PARAMETERS and\n   hardcodes `_TRANSPORT_SRC.read_text()`. Give it an optional `src` parameter so the real guard\n   calls it unchanged and the teeth test feeds it the synthetic source. Do NOT add a second\n   predicate — that reproduces the duplication defect that is sibling ticket bd9e.\n\n--- 79d5's CENTRAL UNKNOWN IS SETTLED, AND MY HYPOTHESIS WAS WRONG ---\nI suspected 32767 was a TEXT-CUSTOM-FIELD limit misapplied to comments, with the comment body an\nunbounded CLOB. THE STORAGE HALF IS RIGHT (`jiraaction.actionbody` is `extremely-long` ->\nLONGTEXT/CLOB) BUT IT IS NOT THE BINDING CONSTRAINT — enforcement is at the APPLICATION layer.\nDecisive primary source: Jira's own `jpm.xml` defines `jira.text.field.character.limit` with\n`default-value 32767` and the description \"Affects Description, Environment, **Comments** and Text\ncustom fields.\" Comments and text custom fields are ONE property by design. Corroborated by\nJRASERVER-28519 (7.0.0: default becomes 32767).\nSO: DO NOT CHANGE THE LIMIT AND DO NOT MARK IT UNVERIFIED — 79d5's AC4 is satisfied by recording\nthis PROVENANCE at the binding. Also settled: Cloud's separate `comment_limits` module exists\nbecause the UNITS differ (plain chars vs ADF-serialized size, `_ADF_DESCRIPTION_LIMIT` = 32000\nfitted empirically because `text_to_adf` inflates ~32767 -> ~50,566), NOT because the numbers differ\n— which corrects an assumption in 79d5's own Context.\nTHE RESIDUAL DEFECT IS NARROWER AND REAL, filed as [rebar:049e-9fac-a821-4ea2] (discovered_from\n79d5): on DC that limit is ADMIN-CONFIGURABLE (0..2147483647) and rebar hardcodes the default, so on\nan instance where an admin raised it rebar truncates comments Jira would have accepted. I\ndeliberately did NOT make it an e369 child — reasoning recorded on the ticket, and it is one command\nto overrule (`rebar edit 049e-9fac-a821-4ea2 --parent e369-a449-4773-48fb`). NOTE: the live harness\ncan NEVER exhibit this bug, because a default-configured instance has exactly 32767.\n\n--- RECOMMENDED ORDER FROM HERE ---\n 1. Land 1209 -> close 727f and d103 (they are finished work sitting on a CI queue).\n 2. Let the queue agent finish 2944 and cc77. Meanwhile certify cedc (retry when `ps` is clear),\n    then bd9e (stale-head only — either re-review, or the handoff-#10 trick: `git checkout -B x\n    338e3973354482e1b2a35d519552afca3e375d98`, claim, rebase forward).\n 3. ONLY WHEN ALL FOUR CHILDREN ARE CERTIFIED, review 3e73 last, then claim the epic and the\n    children (parent-first cascade needs the epic in_progress).\n 4. Implement. FILE SETS ARE DISJOINT except one collision: bd9e AND 79d5 BOTH EDIT\n    `adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py` (bd9e at the ticket-type map ~:43/:61, 79d5 at\n    `sanitize_comment` ~:146). SERIALIZE THOSE TWO BY SCHEDULING — do NOT add a `depends_on` edge,\n    which would fast-fail the other's review and stale attestations.\n 5. 5200 and cc77 are the last two e369 children. e369 itself needs a re-review before it can close\n    (stale-code, 22 files)."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785692684967538001,"uuid":"22379e18-8ea1-46ff-a7d2-2640c6c43cf3"}